Pocket Professor Synopsis
Overview:
Pocket Professor is a Raspberry Pi–based physical AI assistant that helps users troubleshoot IT problems without breaking their focus. Instead of giving direct answers, it provides hint-based voice responses and uses a camera to observe the user's workspace.

Features:
Voice Input & Output — Users speak to the device and receive spoken hints
Camera Vision — Allows the assistant to observe the user's workspace
3D Printed Enclosure — Custom-designed body housing all hardware
Power Management — Maintains reliable operation during sessions

Components — Raspberry Pi 5, Camera, Microphone, Speaker

Sprint 1 — Planning and Setup | Project Manager: Bekah Vanderzee
The team set up Git, initialized documentation, and aligned on project scope. Main challenges were learning Git workflows and getting up to speed on the 3D printer and modeling software.

Sprint 2 — Developing Ideas into Reality | Project Manager: Saige Gee
Active development began with the first two iterations of the code and fitting hardware into the enclosure. The model was scaled up from 8 to 11 inches in height to accommodate the hardware components. Face-down detection was cut from scope due to hardware safety risks, allowing the team to focus on core features.

Sprint 3 — Finishing the Concept | Project Manager: Lukas Gairdner
The team assembled the final model, painted the enclosure, and resolved audio issues; the text-to-speech was running too fast and the volume was too low, both issues were addressed through software adjustments. The AI model's behavior was also stress-tested, with the device remaining functional throughout.
